::: {.doc-subject}

Substitute targeted values in the table body.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/posit-dev/great-tables/blob/main/great_tables/_substitution.py#L379-L478){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.FUNCTION}
```python
GT.sub_values(
    columns=None,
    rows=None,
    values=None,
    pattern=None,
    fn=None,
    replacement=None
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

Should you need to replace specific cell values with custom text, `sub_values()` can be a good
choice. We can target cells for replacement through value, regex, and custom matching rules.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[columns]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [SelectExpr]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The columns to target. Can either be a single column name or a series of column names
    provided in a list.

<code>[rows]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | list[int] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   In conjunction with `columns=`, we can specify which of their rows should be targeted for
    substitution. The default is all rows, resulting in all rows in all targeted columns being
    considered for this substitution. Alternatively, we can supply a list of row indices.

<code>[values]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list[Any] | Any | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The specific value or values that should be replaced with a `replacement` value. If
    `pattern` is also supplied then `values` will be ignored.

<code>[pattern]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   A regex pattern that can target solely those values in character-based columns. If `values`
    is also supplied, `pattern` will take precedence.

<code>[fn]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Callable[..., bool] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   A supplied function that operates on each cell value `x` and should return a boolean
    indicating whether that value should be replaced. If either of `values` or `pattern` is also
    supplied, `fn` will take precedence.

<code>[replacement]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | int | float | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The replacement value for any cell values matched by either `values`, `pattern`, or `fn`.
    Must be a string or numeric value.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [GT]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The GT object is returned. This is the same object that the method is called on so that we
    can facilitate method chaining.
:::

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Let's create an input table with three columns containing an assortment of values that could
potentially undergo some substitution via `sub_values()`.

::: {#5c21b4c7 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
from great_tables import GT
import polars as pl

tbl = pl.DataFrame(
    {
        "num_1": [-0.01, 74.0, None, 0.0, 500.0, 0.001, 84.3],
        "int_1": [1, -100000, 800, 5, None, 1, -32],
        "lett": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F", "G"],
    }
)

GT(tbl).sub_values(values=[74, 500], replacement="—")
```

